            "lens": "review-resilience",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R4-buffered-response",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/hookwire/posttooluse.go:101-102",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "The decoder is documented as streaming tool_response and hashing it as it goes. In fact `dec.Decode(\u0026raw)` into a json.RawMessage buffers the whole response value in memory. The json.Decoder also keeps its own read buffer, and a second copy is made. A response near the 64 MiB bound could therefore take several times that in memory in a short-lived hook process, for every memory read. The bound itself is intact, so this is a memory-cost and documentation mismatch, not a correctness failure.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/hookwire/posttooluse.go:101-102",
                  "engine/hookwire/hookwire.go:58-62"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "introduced"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Read all 12 patches. I found no BLOCKER or CRITICAL resilience defect that this candidate causes.",
              "A failed evidence write degrades to a single additionalContext warning and the hook exits 0. A missing store, a missing clock, an unreadable binding, or a closed workflow all fail silent or warn. They never block the tool call.",
              "Input size is bounded by the LimitReader plus the counting check, so a runaway writer cannot make the hook read without end.",
              "The sidecar adapter and the command wiring are not in this candidate, so I could not check lock contention or retry behavior there."
            ]
          }
        }
